00:03:36.605 --> 00:03:38.365
and they're probably
not technical.

00:03:38.365 --> 00:03:41.570
So they're going to need a more
of a full featured web app.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.890
And these other systems are
gonna interact with some

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.530
programming language that maybe we
don't know now or won't know

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:47.510
about in the future.

00:03:49.545 --> 00:03:50.745
One of my most,

00:03:50.745 --> 00:03:53.145
sort of important principles
when thinking about these types

00:03:53.145 --> 00:03:56.845
of systems is this role,
which is APIs before UIs.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.230
So when we look at
this group of people,

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:03.190
see that there are a few
different groups that are using

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:04.765
something you
might call an API.

00:04:04.765 --> 00:04:08.365
It's a code first interaction
with this product.

00:04:08.365 --> 00:04:10.365
We have these Python users and

00:04:11.485 --> 00:04:13.905
these other systems, which might
use some type of code.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.330
And the reason why I think,

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:20.570
APIs before UIs is such a
good rule is that code interfaces

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:22.675
are way easier to
build than GUI.

00:04:22.675 --> 00:04:23.875
They just have
fewer parameters.

00:04:23.875 --> 00:04:26.915
You don't need to worry
about centering something or making

00:04:26.915 --> 00:04:28.835
the UI kind of intuitive.

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:31.395
You can just build the code
and have people interact with the

00:04:31.395 --> 00:04:33.070
code. Because they're
easier to build,

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:35.410
they're easier to
change and iterate upon.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:38.830
This means that if you're kind
of working mostly on an API,

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:42.015
you're gonna be able to
make more changes more quickly to

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:44.895
center in on the right
interaction, the right data

00:04:44.895 --> 00:04:47.295
model, and the right
type of user flow,

00:04:47.295 --> 00:04:48.755
for your particular product.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.370
And my experience doing this for a
number of years is that a good

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.970
API will usually create a good
UI, both in terms of, like,

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.555
the simplicity of the code,
and also how intuitive it is.

00:04:58.555 --> 00:05:01.195
Once you've kind of
centered on that right, API,

00:05:01.195 --> 00:05:03.595
the right interaction
for your problem,

00:05:03.595 --> 00:05:06.720
the GUI that serves that
type of interaction is pretty

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.920
obvious most of the time.
The opposite is not true.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.400
I've seen a lot of
times people build,

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.735
a GUI first And in doing that,

00:05:14.735 --> 00:05:18.335
they kind of just sort of
throw different things into some type

00:05:18.335 --> 00:05:21.075
of programming data model or
some type of of interface.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.890
And trying to build a
intuitive code interface

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.830
using those same data
structures can be really awkward.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.150
And so if you ever tried
to analyze web data as a data

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.305
scientist, this kind of why.

00:05:31.305 --> 00:05:33.865
You got this big giant blob
of date of JSON that's not

00:05:33.865 --> 00:05:35.085
particularly well structured,

00:05:35.945 --> 00:05:37.485
for your purpose. Right?

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:41.630
So if you ever had the
option of building the code interface

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:45.870
first before doing any type
type of GUI work, I recommend

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.595
doing doesn't always happen, but
in this case, we do. Right?

00:05:48.595 --> 00:05:50.915
We have these these three
groups of people are interested in

00:05:51.955 --> 00:05:54.595
code. They're also probably
our earliest stakeholders,

00:05:54.595 --> 00:05:56.295
so we might wanna
serve them first.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.520
Okay. So one way of
distributing this model is,

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:02.980
to use a Python package. Right?

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.235
I could put this model
in a Python package,

00:06:05.235 --> 00:06:07.335
and have that
package be installed

00:06:07.955 --> 00:06:11.155
from something like Posit
package manager or nexus or

00:06:11.155 --> 00:06:13.220
some other, package
hosting system.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.640
But this creates
a few problems.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.940
There's and I would say
there's maybe there's three main problems.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.345
The first is that packages
are necessarily really hard to

00:06:23.345 --> 00:06:24.725
authenticate and authorize.

00:06:25.265 --> 00:06:27.025
So usually when you look at,

00:06:28.145 --> 00:06:29.045
internal repositories,

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.910
They're like PyPI
like repositories.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.790
They don't have
particularly sophisticated security controls

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.530
on who can download, those packages.

00:06:38.145 --> 00:06:40.625
But the bigger problem is
that once those packages are

00:06:40.625 --> 00:06:42.245
downloaded from
those repositories,

00:06:42.945 --> 00:06:44.065
they're just in the wild.

00:06:44.065 --> 00:06:47.900
There's no real way for to
control who has access to them.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.180
We can't create fine grained
controls, for example,

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.060
give some people access to
one Python function in the package

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.325
and other people access
to another one. And if,

00:06:57.325 --> 00:07:00.285
the individual human who
just downloaded that,

00:07:00.285 --> 00:07:01.985
package shares it
inappropriately

00:07:02.765 --> 00:07:06.340
or leaves their laptop
open and unintended.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.380
Anything that's in there
including the model or any type

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.680
of data that's on that in
that package is gonna be exposed. Right?

00:07:14.425 --> 00:07:17.785
So if we're working on a
model that maybe includes some

00:07:17.785 --> 00:07:21.145
sensitive intellectual property or
might encode some sensitive

00:07:21.145 --> 00:07:23.210
data or expose some
sensitive data,

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:24.970
That's gonna be a really
big problem. Right?

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:28.510
We wanna have the ability to
authenticate the model when it's called.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.125
And the second big problem
with packages is that they always

00:07:32.125 --> 00:07:33.725
require user input to update.

00:07:33.725 --> 00:07:37.725
So if I were to distribute
this model in a, Python package just

00:07:37.725 --> 00:07:41.650
by itself, If I ever changed
the model or changed, you know,

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:44.950
updated it or changed some part
of how that model is called,

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:49.165
I would need to ask all of
my downstream users to update

00:07:49.165 --> 00:07:50.365
their installation.

00:07:50.365 --> 00:07:52.685
And they would need to do
that probably in a lot of places.

00:07:52.685 --> 00:07:54.605
So they may need to update
in all the different virtual

00:07:54.605 --> 00:07:55.965
environments they have
on their computer.

00:07:55.965 --> 00:07:58.320
If they deployed assets
to Posit Connect,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.760
they would need to
update all those.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.380
It becomes an enormous hassle.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.400
And the final reason is
that that would need Python, right,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.295
not probably most of our
users are kind of comfortable using

00:08:07.295 --> 00:08:08.735
Python, but some of
them maybe aren't.

00:08:08.735 --> 00:08:10.735
Maybe have some R
users at my company.

00:08:10.735 --> 00:08:13.540
Maybe there's a Scala group
that wants to call this model.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.720
And creating Python to their

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.600
build environment is maybe
gonna cause some problems.

00:08:20.985 --> 00:08:21.065
An,

00:08:21.065 --> 00:08:23.785
an alternative and what I'm gonna
choose here is to use an

00:08:23.785 --> 00:08:24.985
API. Basically,

00:08:24.985 --> 00:08:27.964
host host this model as an
API on pause on Posit connect.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:32.630
And have, some wrapper
functions that call that API.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:35.350
And this is gonna give
me a couple of major advantages.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:37.805
The first is that
I'm gonna solve,

00:08:37.805 --> 00:08:39.965
I'm gonna solve all three
of the problems that I just

00:08:39.965 --> 00:08:40.365
expressed. Right?

00:08:40.365 --> 00:08:43.165
I'm gonna have to be able
to update that model in one place

00:08:43.165 --> 00:08:45.344
by reuploading it
to Posit Connect.

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:50.220
I will be able to authenticate
access to the API, both the

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:51.980
top level domain,

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:56.385
and also I can authorize any
of the endpoints that people call.

00:08:57.565 --> 00:08:59.725
And finally, it's gonna
be language independent.

00:08:59.725 --> 00:09:02.125
Every programming language has the
ability to wrap and call

00:09:02.125 --> 00:09:05.140
APIs. So if I can put
something up as an API,

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:08.200
then I'm gonna be able to
give access to everybody.

