sophisticated pipelining tool,

00:38:49.090 --> 00:38:53.010
you wanted to use MLflow
or airflow to do model training

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:54.370
validation reporting,

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:58.945
you would still be able to
post from those from those

00:38:58.945 --> 00:39:03.525
pipelines to this API to update
the model or update the data store.

00:39:04.865 --> 00:39:07.240
Finally, if you wanted to host
you're Shiny app somewhere

00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:08.760
else, again, you'd
be able to do that.

00:39:08.760 --> 00:39:12.120
So having these types of
modular pieces will let you

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:14.840
take some swap some of them
in and out without a lot of

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:17.515
refactoring work.
And the term there,

00:39:17.515 --> 00:39:20.475
that's really useful is
called, API contract,

00:39:20.475 --> 00:39:23.435
which is basically the contractual,

00:39:23.435 --> 00:39:25.720
the relationship that you're
promising to where where these

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:27.720
systems plug
together. So, like,

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:30.600
we're exposing some endpoints
to all these different things.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:33.160
And so we're kind of
signing a contract that those endpoints

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:35.605
are gonna be stable. And
you know, maintain their thing,

00:39:35.605 --> 00:39:39.445
even if where it's hosted
or what's happening under the hood

00:39:39.445 --> 00:39:40.405
changes dramatically.

00:39:40.405 --> 00:39:42.880
Those little points of contact
between these different

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:45.060
components of the system
will all be there.

